WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.294
Warsaw Downtown is our real game case study.
A person sets the goal. Coding

2
00:00:04.344 --> 00:00:08.639
agents make changes. Tests and fresh
reviewers challenge the result. The

3
00:00:08.689 --> 00:00:13.033
lesson is how evidence makes
ambitious work safer.

4
00:00:13.033 --> 00:00:17.494
Start even simpler than agents. A model is
software that learns patterns from

5
00:00:17.544 --> 00:00:22.006
examples. A language model generates text
and can suggest code from the

6
00:00:22.056 --> 00:00:26.567
context it receives. Tools around it
provide file access and checks.

7
00:00:26.567 --> 00:00:31.128
An agent is a loop around a model, aimed
toward a goal. It reads context,

8
00:00:31.178 --> 00:00:35.739
chooses an action, uses a tool, observes
what happened, and then tries the

9
00:00:35.789 --> 00:00:40.400
next step. The loop matters because real
engineering needs feedback.

10
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.994
In this project, the agents were development
assistants. They worked on code

11
00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:47.639
and evidence under human direction. The game
characters running inside Warsaw

12
00:00:47.689 --> 00:00:51.333
Downtown are a different system.

13
00:00:51.333 --> 00:00:55.828
The first principle is context. Before
changing files, an agent needs the

14
00:00:55.878 --> 00:01:00.372
project rules, the current code, the visual
target, and Kiran's constraints.

15
00:01:00.422 --> 00:01:04.967
Without that map, fluent code can
still be wrong code.

16
00:01:04.967 --> 00:01:09.028
The second principle is tool use. A useful
coding agent does not just talk. It

17
00:01:09.078 --> 00:01:13.139
searches files, runs checks, opens browser
probes, compares screenshots, and

18
00:01:13.189 --> 00:01:17.300
records what it saw.

19
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:21.772
The project split work into bounded jobs and
separate working copies. One lane

20
00:01:21.822 --> 00:01:26.294
could inspect market stalls. Another could
polish columns. Another could

21
00:01:26.344 --> 00:01:30.867
review multiplayer rules. Smaller
lanes made ownership clearer.

22
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:34.339
A handoff protocol said what an agent could
edit, what it must leave alone,

23
00:01:34.389 --> 00:01:37.861
and what proof it had to bring back. That
kind of boring contract is what lets

24
00:01:37.911 --> 00:01:41.433
dramatic-looking work stay controlled.

25
00:01:41.433 --> 00:01:45.028
Reviewers were meant to see the changed
code, the project rules, and the

26
00:01:45.078 --> 00:01:48.672
review rubric with fresh context. They did
not have to trust the builder's

27
00:01:48.722 --> 00:01:52.367
private story about why the
change was probably fine.

28
00:01:52.367 --> 00:01:56.539
The review used lenses: correctness,
multiplayer safety, rendering cost,

29
00:01:56.589 --> 00:02:00.761
tests, conventions, assets, and visual
quality. Each lens asked a different

30
00:02:00.811 --> 00:02:05.033
question, because one big yes can
hide many small noes.

31
00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:09.072
When a serious problem was reported,
skeptic agents tried to challenge the

32
00:02:09.122 --> 00:02:13.161
failure scenario. Confirmed serious
problems blocked progress. Uncertain

33
00:02:13.211 --> 00:02:17.300
findings stayed uncertain instead of
being dressed up as proof.

34
00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:21.006
One gate lesson was especially important. If
a required reviewer failed, or a

35
00:02:21.056 --> 00:02:24.761
serious verification was incomplete, the
gate had to stop. Incomplete review

36
00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:28.567
is not proof of safety.

37
00:02:28.567 --> 00:02:32.828
Here is a real visual lesson. A column
improvement could look reasonable in

38
00:02:32.878 --> 00:02:37.139
code while the decorative grooves ended up
hidden behind flat front panels.

39
00:02:37.189 --> 00:02:41.500
The fix needed geometry checks and browser
views, not just confident text.

40
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.683
A later fourth of October column comparison
shows a refinement in an isolated

41
00:02:44.733 --> 00:02:47.917
branch. The images help explain the
direction of the work. Version

42
00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:51.150
fingerprints identify what was inspected;
they do not prove beauty by

43
00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:54.433
themselves.

44
00:02:54.433 --> 00:02:58.617
The market work added richer stalls,
containers, lighting, and gameplay

45
00:02:58.667 --> 00:03:02.850
footage. The video capture is real browser
capture. Its encoded frame rate is

46
00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:07.133
not the same thing as a universal
game performance claim.

47
00:03:07.133 --> 00:03:11.594
Performance evidence was treated carefully.
Browser timing, graphics processor

48
00:03:11.644 --> 00:03:16.106
identity, graphics work time, and how
smooth the game feels are related

49
00:03:16.156 --> 00:03:20.667
signals, not the same signal. A
bad sample was rejected.

50
00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:25.172
Tests ask whether logic works. Browser
probes ask whether the game loads and

51
00:03:25.222 --> 00:03:29.728
responds. Screenshots ask whether it looks
right. Performance probes ask

52
00:03:29.778 --> 00:03:34.333
whether the timing evidence is valid.
None replaces the others.

53
00:03:34.333 --> 00:03:38.094
The human role did not disappear. Kiran set
taste, scope, risk tolerance, and

54
00:03:38.144 --> 00:03:41.906
the bar for realism. The agents supplied
drafts, checks, and repairs inside

55
00:03:41.956 --> 00:03:45.767
that direction.

56
00:03:45.767 --> 00:03:49.817
Current inspected game bots use behavior
trees and game input logic. They are

57
00:03:49.867 --> 00:03:53.917
game characters. Development agents helped
build the project. They may share

58
00:03:53.967 --> 00:03:58.067
the word agent, but they are
different systems.

59
00:03:58.067 --> 00:04:02.317
Kiran's next planned experiment is agentic
game bots. The experiment could let

60
00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:06.617
models choose actions under shared game
rules. That is future work, and it

61
00:04:06.667 --> 00:04:10.967
would need fairness, latency, and cost
checks before acceptance.

62
00:04:10.967 --> 00:04:14.528
The pattern is simple: define the job,
isolate the working copy, make the

63
00:04:14.578 --> 00:04:18.139
smallest useful change, test it, review it
cold, challenge the review, and

64
00:04:18.189 --> 00:04:21.800
keep the evidence.

65
00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:26.000
That is agentic engineering at its best:
not full autonomy, not instant

66
00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:30.300
perfection, but uncertainty made visible
and work forced through evidence.

67
00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:34.583
The website opens the same story as an
interactive city: principles, examples,

68
00:04:34.633 --> 00:04:38.967
sources, and the checks that made the
Warsaw workflow more trustworthy.


